=head2 SDL_LockSurface

  my $result = SDL_LockSurface($surface);

C: C<< _Bool SDL_LockSurface(SDL_Surface * surface) >>

Set up a surface for directly accessing the pixels.

Between calls to SDL_LockSurface() / SDL_UnlockSurface(), you can write to and read from `surface->pixels`, using the pixel format stored in `surface->format`. Once you are done accessing the surface, you should use SDL_UnlockSurface() to release it.

Not all surfaces require locking. If `SDL_MUSTLOCK(surface)` evaluates to 0, then you can read and write to the surface at any time, and the pixel format of the surface will not change.

This function can be called on different threads with different surfaces. The locking referred to by this function is making the pixels available for direct access, not thread-safe locking.

=over

=item surface - SDL3::Surface object or undef. the SDL_Surface structure to be locked.

=back

Returns boolean: true on success or false on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

