=head2 SDL_ConvertSurface

  my $result = SDL_ConvertSurface($surface, $format);

C: C<< SDL_Surface * SDL_ConvertSurface(SDL_Surface * surface, SDL_PixelFormat format) >>

Copy an existing surface to a new surface of the specified format.

This function is used to optimize images for faster *repeat* blitting. This is accomplished by converting the original and storing the result as a new surface. The new, optimized surface can then be used as the source for future blits, making them faster.

If you are converting to an indexed surface and want to map colors to a palette, you can use SDL_ConvertSurfaceAndColorspace() instead.

If the original surface has alternate images, the new surface will have a reference to them as well.

This function can be called on different threads with different surfaces.

=over

=item surface - SDL3::Surface object or undef. the existing SDL_Surface structure to convert.

=item format - integer. the new pixel format.

=back

Returns SDL3::Surface object or undef: the new SDL_Surface structure that is created or NULL on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

