=head2 SDL_StretchSurface

  my $result = SDL_StretchSurface($src, $srcrect, $dst, $dstrect, $scaleMode);

C: C<< _Bool SDL_StretchSurface(SDL_Surface * src, const SDL_Rect * srcrect, SDL_Surface * dst, const SDL_Rect * dstrect, SDL_ScaleMode scaleMode) >>

Perform a stretched pixel copy from one surface to another.

Only one thread should be using the `src` and `dst` surfaces at any given time.

=over

=item src - SDL3::Surface object or undef. the SDL_Surface structure to be copied from.

=item srcrect - SDL3::Rect object or undef. the SDL_Rect structure representing the rectangle to be copied, or NULL to copy the entire surface.

=item dst - SDL3::Surface object or undef. the SDL_Surface structure that is the blit target.

=item dstrect - SDL3::Rect object or undef. the SDL_Rect structure representing the target rectangle in the destination surface, or NULL to fill the entire destination surface.

=item scaleMode - integer. the SDL_ScaleMode to be used.

=back

Returns boolean: true on success or false on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.4.0.

=head2 SDL_BlitSurfaceTiled

  my $result = SDL_BlitSurfaceTiled($src, $srcrect, $dst, $dstrect);

C: C<< _Bool SDL_BlitSurfaceTiled(SDL_Surface * src, const SDL_Rect * srcrect, SDL_Surface * dst, const SDL_Rect * dstrect) >>

Perform a tiled blit to a destination surface, which may be of a different format.

The pixels in `srcrect` will be repeated as many times as needed to completely fill `dstrect`.

Only one thread should be using the `src` and `dst` surfaces at any given time.

=over

=item src - SDL3::Surface object or undef. the SDL_Surface structure to be copied from.

=item srcrect - SDL3::Rect object or undef. the SDL_Rect structure representing the rectangle to be copied, or NULL to copy the entire surface.

=item dst - SDL3::Surface object or undef. the SDL_Surface structure that is the blit target.

=item dstrect - SDL3::Rect object or undef. the SDL_Rect structure representing the target rectangle in the destination surface, or NULL to fill the entire surface.

=back

Returns boolean: true on success or false on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_BlitSurfaceTiledWithScale

  my $result = SDL_BlitSurfaceTiledWithScale($src, $srcrect, $scale, $scaleMode, $dst, $dstrect);

C: C<< _Bool SDL_BlitSurfaceTiledWithScale(SDL_Surface * src, const SDL_Rect * srcrect, float scale, SDL_ScaleMode scaleMode, SDL_Surface * dst, const SDL_Rect * dstrect) >>

Perform a scaled and tiled blit to a destination surface, which may be of a different format.

The pixels in `srcrect` will be scaled and repeated as many times as needed to completely fill `dstrect`.

Only one thread should be using the `src` and `dst` surfaces at any given time.

=over

=item src - SDL3::Surface object or undef. the SDL_Surface structure to be copied from.

=item srcrect - SDL3::Rect object or undef. the SDL_Rect structure representing the rectangle to be copied, or NULL to copy the entire surface.

=item scale - number. the scale used to transform srcrect into the destination rectangle, e.g. a 32x32 texture with a scale of 2 would fill 64x64 tiles.

=item scaleMode - integer. scale algorithm to be used.

=item dst - SDL3::Surface object or undef. the SDL_Surface structure that is the blit target.

=item dstrect - SDL3::Rect object or undef. the SDL_Rect structure representing the target rectangle in the destination surface, or NULL to fill the entire surface.

=back

Returns boolean: true on success or false on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_BlitSurface9Grid

  my $result = SDL_BlitSurface9Grid($src, $srcrect, $left_width, $right_width, $top_height, $bottom_height, $scale, $scaleMode, $dst, $dstrect);

C: C<< _Bool SDL_BlitSurface9Grid(SDL_Surface * src, const SDL_Rect * srcrect, int left_width, int right_width, int top_height, int bottom_height, float scale, SDL_ScaleMode scaleMode, SDL_Surface * dst, const SDL_Rect * dstrect) >>

Perform a scaled blit using the 9-grid algorithm to a destination surface, which may be of a different format.

The pixels in the source surface are split into a 3x3 grid, using the different corner sizes for each corner, and the sides and center making up the remaining pixels. The corners are then scaled using `scale` and fit into the corners of the destination rectangle. The sides and center are then stretched into place to cover the remaining destination rectangle.

Only one thread should be using the `src` and `dst` surfaces at any given time.

=over

=item src - SDL3::Surface object or undef. the SDL_Surface structure to be copied from.

=item srcrect - SDL3::Rect object or undef. the SDL_Rect structure representing the rectangle to be used for the 9-grid, or NULL to use the entire surface.

=item left_width - integer. the width, in pixels, of the left corners in `srcrect`.

=item right_width - integer. the width, in pixels, of the right corners in `srcrect`.

=item top_height - integer. the height, in pixels, of the top corners in `srcrect`.

=item bottom_height - integer. the height, in pixels, of the bottom corners in `srcrect`.

=item scale - number. the scale used to transform the corner of `srcrect` into the corner of `dstrect`, or 0.0f for an unscaled blit.

=item scaleMode - integer. scale algorithm to be used.

=item dst - SDL3::Surface object or undef. the SDL_Surface structure that is the blit target.

=item dstrect - SDL3::Rect object or undef. the SDL_Rect structure representing the target rectangle in the destination surface, or NULL to fill the entire surface.

=back

Returns boolean: true on success or false on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_MapSurfaceRGB

  my $result = SDL_MapSurfaceRGB($surface, $r, $g, $b);

C: C<< Uint32 SDL_MapSurfaceRGB(SDL_Surface * surface, Uint8 r, Uint8 g, Uint8 b) >>

Map an RGB triple to an opaque pixel value for a surface.

This function maps the RGB color value to the specified pixel format and returns the pixel value best approximating the given RGB color value for the given pixel format.

If the surface has a palette, the index of the closest matching color in the palette will be returned.

If the surface pixel format has an alpha component it will be returned as all 1 bits (fully opaque).

If the pixel format bpp (color depth) is less than 32-bpp then the unused upper bits of the return value can safely be ignored (e.g., with a 16-bpp format the return value can be assigned to a Uint16, and similarly a Uint8 for an 8-bpp format).

This function can be called on different threads with different surfaces.

=over

=item surface - SDL3::Surface object or undef. the surface to use for the pixel format and palette.

=item r - unsigned integer. the red component of the pixel in the range 0-255.

=item g - unsigned integer. the green component of the pixel in the range 0-255.

=item b - unsigned integer. the blue component of the pixel in the range 0-255.

=back

Returns unsigned integer: a pixel value.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_MapSurfaceRGBA

  my $result = SDL_MapSurfaceRGBA($surface, $r, $g, $b, $a);

C: C<< Uint32 SDL_MapSurfaceRGBA(SDL_Surface * surface, Uint8 r, Uint8 g, Uint8 b, Uint8 a) >>

Map an RGBA quadruple to a pixel value for a surface.

This function maps the RGBA color value to the specified pixel format and returns the pixel value best approximating the given RGBA color value for the given pixel format.

If the surface pixel format has no alpha component the alpha value will be ignored (as it will be in formats with a palette).

If the surface has a palette, the index of the closest matching color in the palette will be returned.

If the pixel format bpp (color depth) is less than 32-bpp then the unused upper bits of the return value can safely be ignored (e.g., with a 16-bpp format the return value can be assigned to a Uint16, and similarly a Uint8 for an 8-bpp format).

This function can be called on different threads with different surfaces.

=over

=item surface - SDL3::Surface object or undef. the surface to use for the pixel format and palette.

=item r - unsigned integer. the red component of the pixel in the range 0-255.

=item g - unsigned integer. the green component of the pixel in the range 0-255.

=item b - unsigned integer. the blue component of the pixel in the range 0-255.

=item a - unsigned integer. the alpha component of the pixel in the range 0-255.

=back

Returns unsigned integer: a pixel value.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_ReadSurfacePixel

  my $result = SDL_ReadSurfacePixel($surface, $x, $y, $r, $g, $b, $a);

C: C<< _Bool SDL_ReadSurfacePixel(SDL_Surface * surface, int x, int y, Uint8 * r, Uint8 * g, Uint8 * b, Uint8 * a) >>

Retrieves a single pixel from a surface.

This function prioritizes correctness over speed: it is suitable for unit tests, but is not intended for use in a game engine.

Like SDL_GetRGBA, this uses the entire 0..255 range when converting color components from pixel formats with less than 8 bits per RGB component.

This function can be called on different threads with different surfaces.

=over

=item surface - SDL3::Surface object or undef. the surface to read.

=item x - integer. the horizontal coordinate, 0 < = x < width.

=item y - integer. the vertical coordinate, 0 < = y < height.

=item r - reference to a scalar holding unsigned integer, pointer object or undef. a pointer filled in with the red channel, 0-255, or NULL to ignore this channel.

=item g - reference to a scalar holding unsigned integer, pointer object or undef. a pointer filled in with the green channel, 0-255, or NULL to ignore this channel.

=item b - reference to a scalar holding unsigned integer, pointer object or undef. a pointer filled in with the blue channel, 0-255, or NULL to ignore this channel.

=item a - reference to a scalar holding unsigned integer, pointer object or undef. a pointer filled in with the alpha channel, 0-255, or NULL to ignore this channel.

=back

Returns boolean: true on success or false on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

